    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "we tried increasing the pressure and adjusting the dwell time like u said but it didnt really change the pattern. it's still just these weird patches where the color is basically gone. it doesn't feel like a mechanical problem because the machine is working fine. is there any chance of some kind of chemical reaction or something in the air or the fabric itself that's just making the ink disappear into nothing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While chemical degradation is rare, moisture trapped within the fabric blend or heavy starch residues can act as a chemical barrier, preventing dye molecules from bonding with the polyester fibers. If your paper stock has a glaze layer or is too glossy, the ink may be sitting on top and recoiling when the pressure creates friction, effectively \"wiping\" the design into the unplastered areas. It could also be a pH imbalance in your ink or water bottle; if your ink contains pH stabilizers, missing or incorrect stabilizer treatment could alter the ink's chemical properties and reduce adhesion.",
      "source": "target",
